WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:04.440
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:09.480
Researchers at NVIDIA have been hitting it
out of the park lately, and this work is no

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:10.480
different.

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:14.679
This technique performs image translation,
which means that the input is an image of

00:00:14.679 --> 00:00:19.750
our choice, and the output is the same image
with somewhat different semantics.

00:00:19.750 --> 00:00:25.440
For instance, an image of a city can be translated
to a map of this city, or a daytime photo

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:30.330
or video can be converted to appear as if
it were shot during the night.

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:33.930
And throughout the video, you'll see so much
more of these exciting applications.

00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:39.750
A typical way of accomplishing this is done
by using Generative Adversarial Networks.

00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:45.670
This is a pair of neural networks where the
generator network creates new synthetic images

00:00:45.670 --> 00:00:51.660
trying to fool a discriminator network which
learns to tell a fake, synthesized image from

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:52.699
a real one.

00:00:52.699 --> 00:00:57.090
These two neural networks learn together,
where one tries to come up with better solutions

00:00:57.090 --> 00:01:02.960
to fool the discriminator, where the discriminator
seeks to get better at telling forgeries from

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:04.489
the real photographs.

00:01:04.489 --> 00:01:09.490
In the end, this rivalry makes both of them
get better and better and the final result

00:01:09.490 --> 00:01:13.600
is an excellent technique to create convincing
image translations.

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:18.200
In this work, not two, but 6 of these networks
are being used, so make sure to have a look

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:19.880
at the paper for details.

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:24.470
There was an earlier work that was able to
perform image translation by leaning on a

00:01:24.470 --> 00:01:27.490
novel cycle consistency constraint.

00:01:27.490 --> 00:01:32.159
This means that we assume that the source
image can be translated to the target image

00:01:32.159 --> 00:01:37.830
and then this target image can be translated
back to look exactly like the source.

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:42.810
This kinda means that these translations are
not arbitrary and are mathematically meaningful

00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:43.810
operations.

00:01:43.810 --> 00:01:49.060
Here, the new technique builds on a novel
assumption that there exists a latent space

00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:53.189
in which the input and output images can both
coexist.

00:01:53.189 --> 00:01:58.960
This latent space is basically an intuitive
and concise representation of some more complicated

00:01:58.960 --> 00:01:59.960
data.

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:04.240
For instance, earlier, we experimented a bit
with fonts and had seen that even though the

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:10.259
theory of font design is not easy, we can
create a 2 dimensional latent space that encodes

00:02:10.259 --> 00:02:16.140
simple properties like curvature that can
describe many many fonts in an intuitive manner.

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:21.010
Remarkably, with this new work, converting
dogs and cats into different breeds is also

00:02:21.010 --> 00:02:21.820
a possibility.

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:33.720
Interestingly, it can also perform real to
synthetic image translation and vice versa.

00:02:33.730 --> 00:02:39.300
So that means that it can create video game
footage from our real world videos, and even

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:44.310
more remarkably, convert video game footage
to real world video.

00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:48.420
This is insanity, one of the craziest ideas
I've seen in a while.

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:49.420
Bravo.

00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:55.240
And now, hold on to your papers because it
can also perform attribute-based image translation.

00:02:55.240 --> 00:02:59.620
This means that for instance, we can grab
an image of a human face and transform the

00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:04.140
model's hair to blonde, add sunglasses or
smiles to it at will.

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:09.690
A limitation of this technique is that training
is still non-trivial as it still relies on

00:03:09.690 --> 00:03:14.560
generative adversarial networks, and it is
not yet clear whether there is a point to

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:17.040
which the training converges or not.

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:19.790
The source code of this project is also available.

00:03:19.790 --> 00:03:23.840
Make sure to take a good look at the license
before doing anything because it is under

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:28.080
the creative commons non-commercial and no
derivatives license.

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:48.139
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

